isofill (0.11.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The second pass is solved, not left part way. It was one coarse-to-fine
    pass of the pyramid, 24 sweeps a level, and stopped there. A coarse cell
    is fixed at the mean of the fixed cells under it, so a coast drawn as a 0
    contour beside high ground is a coarse coast at half the land's height,
    and the sweeps could not take back what that put into the ground beyond:
    on zone-ellarca 100 to 329 m of ground standing in the sea, in strips
    whose whole boundary is zero, and a 54 m dent inside a closed 2350 m
    ring. Against an exact solve it was 3 to 12 m out on tidied ground.
  * Multigrid cycles now follow the pyramid - the residual gathered down the
    same levels, a correction solved there and carried back up bilinearly,
    scaled by the step that minimises the error along it - until a cycle
    moves no cell by 3 mm, or three cycles together take out less than a
    tenth: wide flat basins converge slowly, and there the ground is already
    within a few centimetres. Within 4 mm of an exact solve on gobras and
    ellarca at 3", and 5.5 cm at 1" on ellarca's 2x2.
  * The same answer to the bit on any number of threads: the sweeps are
    red-black, and the line search sums tile by tile and adds the tiles in
    order.
  * Faster on small boxes and slower on whole rasters. A loop over a level
    under 65,536 cells runs on one thread: starting a team for a level a few
    dozen cells across was most of the second pass on a small box, which is
    the editor's preview. Against 0.10.1, interleaved in one process: a 200
    cell box at 3" 0.16 times the time, a 480 cell box at 1" 1.3; whole
    rasters 1.5 times at 3" on gobras, 4 at 1", and about 17 on ellarca's
    2x2 at 1".
  * The output changes, everywhere the second pass fills. tests/pass2_solved
    asks it of three cases - the coast strip, a ring's floor, random ground
    against a solve in double precision - and make check runs it.
  * The second pass's bands, out of core, are sized for the cycles' memory:
    a third again for the pyramid and a third of a float for the right
    sides. In core the peak is unchanged, those coming after the masks go.

 -- Lee Kindness <wangi@opengeofiction.net>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 12:42:20 +0100

isofill (0.10.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The first pass walks a line of sight only to a constraint that can change
    its answer. A cell's value uses the nearest cell in sight of each distinct
    level, so a constraint at a level already seen nearer - most of them, on
    a contour - is not looked at, and nor is a new level farther than all
    sixty-four held. Only the cells which carry a value are visited, a row of
    the search circle at a time, by a table of how far each cell is from the
    next constraint along its row; the constraint flag it replaces was a byte
    too, so it costs no memory.
  * The same answer to the bit: tests/pass1_ref keeps the walk it replaced
    and compares the two on every cell of random rasters, the level cap
    among them, and make check runs it. Over danu's sets the binary's output
    is identical, and faster: ellarca at 3" 5.21 to 2.73 s, N20E086 at 1"
    41.3 to 15.4 s, N20E087 at 1" 30.4 to 16.7 s.

 -- Lee Kindness <wangi@opengeofiction.net>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 09:56:57 +0100

isofill (0.10.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A threads of zero means all but two in the library as well as the command
    line. The resolution lived in main(), so the binary left two cores free
    and every library caller quietly took every one - which on a desktop is
    the machine somebody is working on. isofill_resolve_threads is that
    arithmetic in one place and both read it. The binary is unchanged: it
    still prints "14 of 16 cores" and --threads N still wins.
  * A 77.8 M cell fill on a sixteen core laptop: all cores 92.9 s, all but two
    98.9, half 120.3, four 158.9. Two cores of headroom cost six and a half
    per cent, where the comment in main() guessed thirty - thirty is about
    what half the cores cost.
  * Zero no longer means "leave the runtime's setting alone", which it did
    before: the fill sets the count on every call. A host with its own count
    should pass that number.

 -- Lee Kindness <wangi@opengeofiction.net>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 20:20:34 +0100

isofill (0.9.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * isofill_diffuse(): the second pass on its own, for a caller that wants to
    re-solve a box out of a larger surface rather than the whole raster. The
    boundary is expressed by writing values into the surface, which the solve
    already treats as fixed, so there is no new concept in the API - only the
    pass that was not reachable before.
  * The second pass reports a failed allocation instead of printing and
    calling exit, so a library caller is told rather than killed. The binary
    still dies loudly, which is right for a batch run. The first pass is
    unchanged and still exits.

