isofill (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A library. libisofill exposes the in-core fill as isofill_run(), arrays
    in and an array out, and the binary's own in-core path now calls it, so
    what the library returns and what the binary writes cannot differ. The
    first pass's per-cell loop is one function shared by the in-core call
    and the out-of-core banding. The surface is unchanged: danu's golden
    reference, built with 0.5.0, still matches cell for cell.
  * isofill --version, and isofill_version(), so a caller can check.
  * make check runs the binary and a small C program against the library.
  * The package installs the library and header beside the binary.

 -- Lee Kindness <wangi@opengeofiction.net>  Sat, 19 Sep 2026 10:30:00 +0000

isofill (0.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Elevations are floats. Both passes worked in short and whole metres, and
    both rounded - the first every value it interpolated between two contours,
    the second everything it filled. A hillshade is a derivative, so that drew
    a faint ring around every contour and nothing downstream could put back
    what had been discarded. Output is Float32; the sentinels keep their values
    rather than becoming NaN, being exactly representable and exactly
    comparable.
  * The second pass solves Laplace's equation. The linear pass it replaces
    interpolated along every row and column and blended the two, which is
    continuous in height and not in slope: the surface is piecewise linear, so
    its derivative jumps at every anchor and each joint drew an axis-aligned
    edge. On ground the first pass cannot answer that read as rectangular
    blocks rather than as landform. Same runtime, less memory.
  * The linear pass is gone, with --pass2 and --pass2-tile. Tiling was a linear
    concern - it bounded how far a value carried along a row - and tiling
    diffusion would seam.
  * Three sets of cells are held at zero for the solve: outside --mask, the new
    --water, and out-of-reach ground that floods to where the contours stop.
    Laplace has no notion of running out of information, so without the last
    an unconstrained region takes the mean of its whole boundary.
  * --grad-min defaults to 0.02, not 0.1. It is a distance like --radius, and
    was never scaled when the grid went from 3 arcseconds to 1, so it asked
    three times the slope the original did.
  * The two passes decide banding separately. A banded first pass is exact; a
    banded second is an approximation, so where there is room for the solve but
    not for the first pass's summed area table, it is taken.
  * Above --max-mem the second pass now has an out-of-core solve rather than
    refusing. It is an approximation and says so.

 -- Lee Kindness <lee@leekindness.com>  Thu, 03 Sep 2026 14:59:12 +0100

isofill (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The second pass now fills the cells the first pass found nothing for, the
    same as those it found too little for, and --no-reach is accepted and
    ignored. This is what the original does: TileUtil's radius_value has one
    failure return, and finding nothing reaches it exactly as finding too
    little does. The old split reset those cells to zero after the second
    pass, copied from gdal_fillnodata -md rather than from TileUtil, and put
    114,309 cells of zone-tapira at 1 m.
  * Verified against 0.3.1 run with --no-reach on zone-tapira's constraint
    raster: 0 differing cells of 51,854,401.
  * The OUT_OF_REACH sentinel is unchanged and --no-pass2 still writes it
    through. Telling "saw nothing" from "saw too little" is how the above was
    found and stays worth having.

 -- Lee Kindness <lee.kindness@inovageo.com>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 23:34:39 +0100

isofill (0.3.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix an out of memory on wide zones. The windowed second pass sized its
    buffer as (tile + 2 * margin) squared, so a whole-raster tile on axian's
    64801x32401 asked for 8.6 GB rather than 4.3 GB, and 0.3.0 routed every
    large zone through it because the streaming pass could not honour --reach.
  * The streaming second pass now honours it, so large zones take that path
    again. Verified identical to the in-memory path on alved, 0 cells of
    51,854,401 differing.

 -- Lee Kindness <wangi@opengeofiction.net>  Thu, 27 Aug 2026 00:30:00 +0000

isofill (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The second pass no longer carries a value into cells the first pass had no
    constraint within reach of; they stay zero, as gdal_fillnodata -md leaves
    them. On alved that takes water beyond the radius from 11.54% to 0.00%.
  * --barrier widens constraints for the sight test only, not for their values,
    since a one cell line at 1 arcsecond is a third of the wall it was at 3.
  * --pass2-tile bounds how far the second pass carries a value. Off by default.

 -- Lee Kindness <wangi@opengeofiction.net>  Wed, 26 Aug 2026 23:30:00 +0000

isofill (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Work band by band above --max-mem, so a whole zone at 1 arcsecond fits:
    the summed area table alone is 42 GB for an 86401x39601 raster.
  * --mask bounds where cells may be filled, without narrowing what any cell
    can see, so a zone-wide run no longer invents terrain across unmapped
    squares.
  * Default to all but two cores rather than every core.

